steps.body:
BFS explores a grid in rings: first the start, then every cell one step away, then two steps away, and so on. A queue makes that happen. Cells go in the back with `offer` and come out the front with `poll`, so nearer cells always come out first.

```java
static final int[][] DIRS = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};

char[][] grid = {"..#".toCharArray(), ".#.".toCharArray(), "...".toCharArray()};
int rows = grid.length;
int cols = grid[0].length;
boolean[][] visited = new boolean[rows][cols];
int startRow = 0;
int startCol = 0;

Queue<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
queue.offer(new int[]{startRow, startCol});
visited[startRow][startCol] = true;
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int[] cur = queue.poll();
    System.out.println(cur[0] + "," + cur[1]);
    for (int[] d : DIRS) {
        int nr = cur[0] + d[0];
        int nc = cur[1] + d[1];
        if (nr < 0 || nr >= rows || nc < 0 || nc >= cols
                || grid[nr][nc] == '#' || visited[nr][nc]) {
            continue; // skip out of bounds, walls, and visited cells
        }
        visited[nr][nc] = true;
        queue.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
    }
}
```

Each queue entry is an `int[]` of `{row, col}`. `DIRS` lists the four moves (down, up, right, left), so one loop handles every neighbor and the visit order is fixed.

Mark a cell visited when you **enqueue** it, not when you poll it. If you wait until the poll, two neighbors can both add the same cell before it comes out, and it gets processed twice. Never `println` an `int[]` or a list of them: print `cur[0] + "," + cur[1]`.

