steps.body:
To measure distance, replace the `boolean[][] visited` grid of a plain BFS with an `int[][] dist` that stores how many steps each cell is from the start. Fill it with `-1` to mean "not reached yet", one row at a time:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int rows = 3;
        int cols = 4;
        int sr = 0;
        int sc = 0;
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(dist));
    }
}
```

```java
int[][] dist = new int[rows][cols];
for (int[] row : dist) {
    Arrays.fill(row, -1);
}
dist[sr][sc] = 0;
```

`Arrays.fill(dist, -1)` compiles, but `dist` holds row arrays, not ints, so it throws `ArrayStoreException` at run time. Fill each row instead.

When you enqueue a neighbor `(nr, nc)` of the polled cell `cur`, it is one step farther than `cur`:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[][] dist = {{0, -1}, {-1, -1}};
        int[] cur = {0, 0};
        int nr = 1;
        int nc = 0;
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(Arrays.deepToString(dist));
    }
}
```

```java
dist[nr][nc] = dist[cur[0]][cur[1]] + 1;
```

`dist[nr][nc] != -1` now doubles as the visited check. Because BFS reaches cells in order of distance, the first time it touches the target is the shortest path, so you can return as soon as you poll it. If the queue empties first, the target was walled off: return `-1`. This works because every move costs 1. With weighted moves you need a different algorithm.

