```java
int minutes = 0;
while (!queue.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {
    int size = queue.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        int[] cur = queue.poll();
        // spread to neighbors and offer them
    }
    minutes++;
}
```

Save `int size = queue.size();` before the inner loop. The queue grows as you offer neighbors, so `i < queue.size()` would keep going into the next level.

When the spread starts from several cells at once, offer **all** of them before the loop. That is multi-source BFS: every start is level 0, and each cell is reached by the nearest one. Change a cell's character when you enqueue it (`.` to `F`) so it is never added twice, and keep a count of the cells still waiting (`fresh` above). Stopping when that count hits 0 keeps the last minute from counting an empty round. If the queue empties while some remain, they can never be reached: return `-1`.

